= Millet

_A category entry, on the model of fish.typ: "millet" is not a species, a genus, or even a clade but a *grade* — small-seeded annual cereal grasses, domesticated independently at least three times on two continents, and lumped by seed size rather than by kinship. The comparative structure of this entry is therefore unusual, and is its main point: *this is not one substance seen by four traditions, but four different substances occupying one functional slot*. China canonized *粟* and *黍* (foxtail and proso millet, domesticated on the Yellow River); India's saṃhitās canonized *कङ्गु*, *श्यामाक* and *कोद्रव* under the explicitly second-rank heading *कुधान्य*, "inferior grains"; Tibb knows *دخن* (pearl millet, out of the Sahel) and *جاورس*; Greco-Roman pharmacy knows *milium* and *panicum*. Where the traditions converge is striking given that they are describing different plants: *light, dry, astringent, drying, binding, cooling, and second-rank to the prestige grain*. Sorghum (ज्वार, 高粱) is conventionally excluded and is not treated here; Job's tears, sometimes miscalled a millet, has its own entry (coix.typ). Species-specific satellites may follow this entry as salmon and sardines follow fish.typ._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*The category problem, first.* The millets are *polyphyletic*. They sit in at least four tribes of Poaceae, were domesticated independently in *North China* (~8000 BP), the *Sahel* (~4500 BP) and *East Africa*, and are grouped by a shared agronomic and culinary profile — small grain, short season, drought tolerance, poor-soil tolerance — rather than by descent. *No classical tradition had the category in the modern sense*; each had names for the millets it actually grew.

*Principal species, with the tradition that canonized each:*
- *_Setaria italica_ (L.) P.Beauv.* — *foxtail millet*; Chinese *粟 (sù)*, husked *小米 (xiǎomǐ)*; domesticated on the Yellow River loess, with Cishan and Peiligang archaeology from ~8000 BP. *The foundational grain of northern Chinese civilization, earlier than wheat there.*
- *_Panicum miliaceum_ L.* — *proso* or *broomcorn millet*; Chinese *黍 (shǔ)*, glutinous forms *黃米*; also a North Chinese domesticate, and the millet that spread earliest westward across Eurasia.
- *_Cenchrus americanus_ (L.) Morrone* (= *_Pennisetum glaucum_*) — *pearl millet*; Hindi *बाजरा (bājrā)*, Arabic *دُخْن (dukhn)*; domesticated in the *Sahel* and in India by ~4000 BP. The most drought-tolerant cereal in cultivation.
- *_Eleusine coracana_ (L.) Gaertn.* — *finger millet*; Kannada/Hindi *रागी (rāgī)*, Marathi *नाचणी (nāchṇī)*; East African highland origin, early to India. *Exceptionally high in calcium* — see Modern Nutrition.
- *_Paspalum scrobiculatum_ L.* — *kodo millet*; *कोद्रव (kodrava)* — and the one with a genuine toxicity record, classically noted and modernly explained (see Viruddha).
- *_Echinochloa_ spp.* — *barnyard millet*; *श्यामाक (śyāmāka)*, *सावाँ/सामक*; a classical Indian grain and a modern Hindu *vrata* (fasting) food.
- *_Panicum sumatrense_* — little millet, *कुटकी*; *_Eragrostis tef_* — *teff*, Ethiopian, the grain of *injera*, sometimes grouped here.

*English and Latin.* *Millet* ← Old French *millet*, diminutive of *mil* ← Latin *mīlium*. Latin had *two* words for two millets — *mīlium* (proso) and *pānicum* (foxtail) — the latter from *pānus*, "ear of millet; swelling," and the source of the modern genus *Panicum*. *That Latin, Greek and Chinese each maintained two distinct millet words is the clearest evidence that the ancients did not have "millet" as a single category*: they had two crops that we have since merged.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short and de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_ (s.v. _milium_, _panicum_, _panus_); _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "millet"). Domestication dates and sites (Cishan/Peiligang for Chinese millets; Sahelian pearl millet; East African finger millet) follow standard archaeobotanical literature and should be read as approximate and subject to revision.]

*Greek:* *κέγχρος (kénchros)* — proso millet — and *ἔλυμος (élymos)* — foxtail; *Dioscorides treats both separately*, as does Pliny in Latin.

*Chinese — and millet is embedded in the word for the state itself.* *粟 (sù)* foxtail millet, husked *小米*; *黍 (shǔ)* proso; *稷 (jì)*, a millet whose precise identification (foxtail or proso) is debated. *稷* is also *the god of grain*, and *社稷 (shèjì)* — "the god of the soil and the god of millet" — became the standard classical term for *the state, the body politic*. To speak of the fate of the 社稷 was to speak of the dynasty. *A millet is inside the Chinese word for the nation.* Millets are also fixed members of the *五穀 (wǔgǔ,* five grains), whose membership varies across sources but includes 黍 and 稷 in every version. *粱 (liáng)* denotes fine foxtail millet, and *膏粱* ("fat meat and fine millet") is the classical idiom for rich food and, by extension, for the wealthy.#footnote[社 (god of the soil) and 稷 (god of grain/millet) compounding to 社稷 as a term for the state: standard Chinese lexicography (_Hanyu Da Cidian_) and classical usage throughout the pre-Qin and later corpus. The identification of 稷 as foxtail or proso millet is a long-standing philological dispute and is flagged as unresolved. 五穀 membership varies by source — a standard point in Chinese agricultural history.]

*Sanskrit — and the classification is a verdict.* The saṃhitās treat millets within *कुधान्य (kudhānya,* "inferior grains") or *तृणधान्य (tṛṇadhānya,* "grass grains"), as against the prestige *शूकधान्य (śūkadhānya)* class headed by *śāli* rice. Named species: *कङ्गु (kaṅgu)* foxtail; *चीनक (cīnaka)* proso — *literally "the Chinese grain," naming the transmission route, exactly as the Chinese 胡- and 洋- prefixes name theirs*; *श्यामाक (śyāmāka)* barnyard; *कोद्रव (kodrava)* kodo; *नीवार (nīvāra)* a wild grain. *बाजरा* and *रागी* are *post-classical vernaculars* for the African millets; their Sanskrit identifications are uncertain and should not be asserted.

*Arabic / Persian (Tibb):* *دُخْن (dukhn)* — pearl millet; *جَاوَرْس (jāwars)* — proso/common millet; Persian *ارزن (arzan)*. Both *dukhn* and *jāwars* are treated in the Avicennan and Ibn al-Bayṭār corpus as distinct drugs.

*Japanese:* *粟 (awa)* foxtail, *黍 (kibi)* proso — and *黍団子 (kibi-dango)*, the millet dumplings with which Momotarō recruits his companions in the folktale. *Korean:* *조 (jo)* foxtail, *기장 (gijang)* proso; both in *오곡밥 (ogokbap)*, the five-grain rice of the first full moon.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Classical and ancient — millets are the grains northern Chinese medicine was built on, and both 粟米 and 黍米 carry Bencao entries as distinct drugs with *different* properties. The intra-category divergence is the point: one is cooling, the other warming._

*粟米 (sùmǐ, foxtail millet):*
- *Property:* Cool to slightly cold (涼 / 微寒). *Flavor:* Sweet (甘), salty (鹹). *Channels:* Kidney (腎), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃).
- *Actions:* *和中益腎 (hé zhōng yì shèn)* — harmonizes the middle and benefits the Kidney; *除熱解毒 (chú rè jiě dú)* — clears heat and resolves toxicity; *止痢* — checks dysenteric diarrhoea.
- *陳粟米 (chén sùmǐ,* aged foxtail millet) is specified as a *distinct and more cooling* form, preferred for *消渴* (consumptive thirst) and for dysentery — an ageing-as-processing distinction of the kind this book has met in tea and tangerine peel.

*黍米 (shǔmǐ, proso millet):*
- *Property:* *Warm (溫)* — *the opposite pole from 粟米*, and the clearest demonstration that "millet" is not one drug in this tradition. *Flavor:* Sweet (甘). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺).
- *Actions:* *益氣補中 (yì qì bǔ zhōng)* — augments qi and supplements the centre.
- *Caution:* the glutinous forms are classically noted as *difficult to digest* and as *困脾* (encumbering the Spleen) and heat-generating in quantity — 多食 produces vexation and heat.

*The living dietetic preparation — 小米粥 (xiǎomǐ zhōu, millet congee).* This is the canonical Chinese *convalescent, postpartum and infant* food, and the register in which most Chinese people actually meet millet medicinally. The thick glossy film that gathers on the surface of a long-simmered congee — *米油 (mǐyóu,* "rice oil," though the millet version is the prized one) — is specifically singled out in the dietetic literature as the most nourishing part, skimmed and given to the weak and to new mothers. *A folk-dietetic practice of real standing, not a textual prescription*, and flagged as such.

*Cautions:* 粟米's cooling property is cautioned in Spleen-Stomach vacuity cold in quantity; 黍米's warmth and stickiness in damp-heat and in weak digestion.#footnote[粟米 and 黍米 as distinct Bencao drugs with the cooling/warming divergence, and the 陳粟米 aged form: _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grain section (穀部); modern Chinese dietetics references. 小米粥 and 米油 as convalescent and postpartum food: living folk-dietetic practice, widely attested in modern Chinese dietetic literature rather than prescribed in a classical formula — flagged accordingly.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Classical, and classified unflatteringly. Millets are treated in the saṃhitā and nighaṇṭu literature under *कुधान्य (kudhānya)* — "inferior grains" — as against the *śūkadhānya* prestige class headed by rice. This is not a modern slight projected backwards; it is the classical valuation, and it is pharmacologically reasoned rather than merely snobbish: the millets are *laghu* and *rūkṣa* where rice is *guru* and *snigdha*, which makes them reducing rather than building grains._

*Rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — the defining secondary note).

*Guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry) — *the pair that defines the whole class*; Khara/Sthira in some readings.

*Vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) for most of the class — with genuine variation, *kaṅgu* being given as uṣṇa in some sources. *Flagged:* attributions differ by species and by nighaṇṭu, and a blanket vīrya for "millet" is not defensible; the per-species readings should be checked against a specific edition.

*Vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent); madhura in some readings.

*Prabhāva:* *None, and none is warranted.* The actions of the millets follow directly and unremarkably from madhura-kaṣāya rasa, laghu-rūkṣa guṇa and śīta vīrya: a light, dry, astringent, cooling grain binds, reduces, and fails to nourish deeply. Nothing requires a prabhāva-level explanation, and per this book's Prabhāva rule the honest move is to decline rather than to invent one. (*कोद्रव*'s stupefying property, treated under Viruddha, might look like a candidate — but it is a *contamination* effect, not a property of the grain.)

*Doṣa effect:* *Kapha-śāmaka* (laghu-rūkṣa — the governing benefit, and the basis of the reducing register); *Pitta-śāmaka* for the śīta species; *Vāta-vardhaka* — *the classical objection, and the reason for the kudhānya rank*: laghu-rūkṣa-kaṣāya is the canonical Vāta-aggravating combination, so millets are classically unsuitable for the depleted, the emaciated, the aged and the Vāta-constituted, and are to be cooked with fat when used at all.

*Karma:* _Laṅghana_ (reducing — explicitly the opposite of rice's _bṛṃhaṇa_), _rūkṣaṇa_ (drying), _grāhī_ (binding; the classical use in _atīsāra_), _lekhana_ (scraping — the register modern practitioners invoke for metabolic disease), _medohara_ (fat-reducing), _kaphahara_. *Used in famine, in reducing regimens, and by the poor* — the saṃhitās are candid about the social fact.

*A live reversal worth recording.* The classical verdict — *kudhānya*, inferior grain — has been *officially inverted in modern India*: the Government of India rebranded millets *श्री अन्न (Shree Anna,* "the excellent grain") around the UN *International Year of Millets* (2023), as a nutrition-and-climate policy. *The rebranding is a deliberate reversal of a classical classification*, and this book notes it as such rather than pretending the tradition always valued them. Whether the reversal is justified is taken up under Modern Nutrition, where the honest answer is *partly*: on glycaemic index, micronutrients and climate resilience, yes; on nourishing the depleted, the classical reading still stands.#footnote[कुधान्य / तृणधान्य classification of millets against the śūkadhānya prestige grains: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (annapānavidhi, grain sections); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_. Per-species rasa-guṇa-vīrya attributions vary across nighaṇṭus and are flagged for verification. The _Shree Anna_ rebranding and the UN International Year of Millets (2023): Government of India policy documents and FAO materials — a contemporary policy fact, noted as such.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tags: S core saṃhitā, N later nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional:* _doṣa-viruddha_ in Vāta excess and Vāta constitutions — the governing classical caution; _avasthā-viruddha_ in emaciation, convalescence and the aged, where a *laṅghana* grain is the wrong instrument; _mātrā-viruddha_ as a sole staple without fat.
- *(S/N) कोद्रव (kodrava) — a classical toxicity note, and a correct one.* The saṃhitā and nighaṇṭu literature records kodo millet as capable of producing *stupefaction, intoxication and derangement* — a specific warning attached to one species among the class. *Modern toxicology vindicates it precisely:* "kodua poisoning" is caused by *ergot contamination* of _Paspalum scrobiculatum_ by *Claviceps paspali*, producing tremor, vomiting, giddiness and delirium. *The classical observation was right, and misattributed the cause to the grain rather than its fungus* — which is exactly how a pre-microbial pharmacology would record a contamination effect.
- *(F) Folk/regional:* the universal practice of cooking millets *with fat* — ghee on bājrā roti, oil in rāgī preparations, butter in Chinese congee — which is the traditions' own correction of the rūkṣa quality; and the *fermented* preparations (rāgī ambli, millet gruels), which modern food science shows reduce antinutrients.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Goitrogens — the most important modern caution.* Pearl millet and finger millet contain *C-glycosyl flavones* that inhibit *thyroid peroxidase*. In populations where millet is the dominant staple *and* iodine intake is low — parts of the Sahel and of semi-arid India — this contributes measurably to *endemic goitre*. The risk is a *population-level, staple-dependence, iodine-deficiency* phenomenon, not a hazard of eating millet as one grain among several in an iodine-sufficient diet, and conflating the two is a common error in both directions.
  - *Kodo millet ergot* — as above; a sourcing and storage issue, and a reason kodo specifically warrants attention to provenance.
  - *Phytate and tannins* — reduce mineral bioavailability, notably offsetting part of finger millet's remarkable calcium content; *substantially mitigated by soaking, germination and fermentation*, which the traditional preparations all perform.
  - *Gluten-free* — millets contain no gluten-related prolamins and are suitable in coeliac disease (cf. `wheat.typ`, `barley.typ`), subject to the usual cross-contact caveat in shared milling.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Classical. Both *دخن (dukhn,* pearl millet) and *جاورس (jāwars,* proso) are treated as distinct simples in the Avicennan and Ibn al-Bayṭār corpus, and the Tibb reading converges closely with the Ayurvedic one despite concerning a different species._ See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Names:* *دُخْن (dukhn)* — pearl millet, the Sahelian and Egyptian grain; *جَاوَرْس (jāwars)* — proso/common millet; Persian *ارزن (arzan)*; Urdu *باجرا (bājrā)* for the pearl millet, a borrowing from the Indian vernacular.

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry* — commonly given in the *1st–2nd degree*, with the *dryness* emphasized above the coldness. *This converges with the Ayurvedic laghu-rūkṣa-śīta reading of a different species*, which is a genuine cross-system agreement reached independently about a shared agronomic class.

*Afʿāl (actions):* *Qābiḍ* (astringent — the binding action); *mujaffif* (desiccant, strongly); *mughadhdhī qalīl al-ghidhāʾ* — *"nourishing, but of little nourishment"*, which is the classical Arabic assessment and a candid one: millet feeds, but less well than wheat; *mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic).

*Istemālāt (indications):* diarrhoea and dysenteric conditions (the astringent-binding use); as a *food for the febrile and the plethoric*, where its coldness and poor nourishment are advantages; and — *the distinctive external use* — as a *dry hot compress*: millet grain heated and applied in a bag or cloth for colic, pain and cold swellings, a preparation shared with the Greek tradition and *still alive in European folk practice as the German Hirsekissen (millet pillow)*.

*Mawāneʿ (contraindications):* cold and dry (*sawdāwī*) temperaments, in whom a Cold-Dry poor-nourishment grain compounds the imbalance and is held to generate *black bile*; the depleted and the convalescent, for whom the tradition prefers wheat or barley.

*Muṣliḥ (corrective):* *fats and sweets* — milk, butter, oil, honey. *Every millet-eating culture in this entry independently arrived at the same correction*: ghee on the bājrā roti, oil in the rāgī, butter in the congee, milk in the European porridge. *Badal:* *شعير (shaʿīr,* barley) — the nearest cold-dry cereal, see `barley.typ`.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none specifically for dukhn or jāwars.* The Prophetic grain register concerns *barley* (*talbīna*, see the glossary and `barley.typ`) and wheat; millet has no hadith standing, and none should be claimed for it.#footnote[دخن and جاورس in Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II, and Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, as distinct simples; the mizāj attribution (Cold-Dry, with dryness emphasized) and the _qalīl al-ghidhāʾ_ "of little nourishment" assessment are standard in the classical literature and are flagged for verification against a specific edition. The heated-millet dry compress is attested in both the Greek and Arabic traditions and survives as the German _Hirsekissen_.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*小米粥 (millet congee)* and its skimmed *米油* — the convalescent, postpartum and weaning preparation, and the principal living use. *五穀粥* (five-grain congee) as a general dietetic. *陳粟米* (aged foxtail millet) decocted for dysentery and thirst. Millet was also a principal *fermentation* grain of early China — the grain of early *醴 (lǐ,* sweet ale) and of millet wines preceding the rice-wine tradition — a register now almost entirely displaced by rice and sorghum.

=== Ayurveda

Classically, *kudhānya* preparations are simple: gruels, flatbreads and parched grain, taken in reducing regimens, in *jvara* convalescence where lightness is wanted, and in scarcity. The living regional preparations carry the tradition:
- *रागी मुद्दे (ragi mudde)* — stiff finger-millet balls, the staple of southern Karnataka, eaten with sambar.
- *रागी अंबली (ragi ambli)* — a *fermented* finger-millet gruel, drunk cool in summer; the fermentation is the traditional antinutrient correction.
- *बाजरे की रोटी (bājre kī roṭī)* — pearl-millet flatbread of Rajasthan and Gujarat, a *winter* bread eaten with ghee and jaggery (see `jaggery.typ`) — the fat-and-sweet *muṣliḥ* applied without the theory.
- *श्यामाक / सामक* — barnyard millet as a *vrata* (fasting-day) grain, permitted where true cereals are not, alongside amaranth and buckwheat (see `amaranth.typ`, `buckwheat.typ`, and the glossary's _vrata_ entry).

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*Classical, and more substantial than its modern European absence suggests.* *Dioscorides* treats *κέγχρος* and *ἔλυμος* separately, and *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XVIII) discusses millet bread and porridge at length as ordinary Roman food. Two preparations matter:
- *Millet porridge and bread* — a European peasant staple for millennia, and *specifically the grain that maize displaced*: from the 16th century onward, New World maize took over the northern Italian and Balkan porridge niche (*polenta* was millet before it was maize), and millet retreated to fodder. *The disappearance of millet from European diets is a displacement event, not a loss of interest.*
- *The heated-millet compress* — dry grain warmed and applied in a bag for pain and colic, described in the classical sources and surviving continuously in German-speaking folk medicine as the *Hirsekissen*. One of the longer-lived preparations in this book.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

*دخن* and *جاورس* as above: binding preparations for diarrhoea, the dry compress, and millet as a staple bread and porridge grain across the Sahel, Egypt and the Maghreb — *including couscous*, which in much of West Africa and historically in parts of the Maghreb is rolled from *millet* rather than wheat semolina.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

*The millets are the climate-resilient cereals, and this is their defining agronomic fact.* They are *C₄* grasses with low water requirement, short growing season, tolerance of poor and shallow soils, and — in pearl millet's case — the highest heat and drought tolerance of any cereal in cultivation. They grow where wheat, rice and often maize fail. See `climates.typ`.

=== Semi-arid Sahel (pearl millet)

Köppen BSh — 300–600 mm of highly variable rainfall, extreme heat, sandy soils. *Pearl millet is the staple*, and in much of the Sahel there is no alternative cereal: it is grown where sorghum is marginal and maize impossible. Preparations are porridges, thick pastes (*tô*), fermented gruels, and millet couscous. *Constitutionally* this is hot-arid: the grain's cooling-drying reading suits it, and the correctives are the universal ones — milk, fat, groundnut (see `peanut.typ`, whose Sahelian register is the same zone).

=== Semi-arid India (pearl and finger millet)

Rajasthan, Gujarat, the Deccan and Karnataka: the *rainfed* "coarse cereal" zone. *Bājrā* in the hot dry northwest, eaten as a *winter* bread with ghee and jaggery — a seasonal pairing that is exactly the classical correction of a cold-dry grain. *Rāgī* in southern Karnataka. *The Green Revolution displaced both*: subsidized rice and wheat procurement pushed millets off good land and out of the public distribution system from the 1960s, and much of the current policy push is an attempt to reverse that.

=== Temperate and semi-arid North China (foxtail and proso)

The Yellow River loess plateau: cold winters, short season, erratic rainfall. *These millets preceded wheat as the staple of northern Chinese civilization*, and the Bencao attributions and the congee tradition are built on that foundation. Millet remains the northern convalescent grain even where wheat dominates the everyday diet.

=== Highland East Africa and the Horn (finger millet, teff)

Ethiopian and East African highlands: finger millet's origin zone, and teff's. *Injera*, the fermented teff flatbread, is the clearest surviving case of fermentation as the standard preparation for a small grain.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Two transpositions, running in opposite directions. First, *out*: the Green Revolution and maize together removed millets from diets across Europe, India and much of Africa within a century, replacing a drought-adapted local grain with irrigated or imported ones — *a deśa-level substitution whose climatic bill is now being presented*. Second, *back in*: the "ancient grain" and "superfood" repositioning has returned millets to affluent diets as a premium health food, at prices and in forms (puffed snacks, flours, milks) remote from any traditional preparation, and generally *without* the fat, fermentation or soaking that every traditional register treated as constitutive. *The classical correction has been dropped in the course of the revival* — which is the recurring pattern of this book's Modern transposition sections, and nowhere clearer than here. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition varies substantially *by species*, so a single profile would mislead; approximate per 100 g, raw, and *verify against USDA FoodData Central and Indian food-composition tables*, which differ.

- *General pattern:* Energy ~340–380 kcal; protein ~7–12 g; fat ~1.5–5 g; carbohydrate ~65–75 g; dietary fibre ~8 g; good magnesium, phosphorus, B vitamins; *gluten-free*; low-to-moderate glycaemic index relative to polished rice.
- *Finger millet (rāgī) — the standout:* *calcium ~344 mg per 100 g*, by a wide margin the highest of any common cereal and roughly ten times that of rice or wheat. *Qualified:* phytate and the grain's own oxalate reduce absorption, so the figure overstates what is bioavailable — though *germination and fermentation substantially improve it*, which is precisely what the traditional *ambli* and malted preparations do.
- *Pearl millet (bājrā):* comparatively high in *iron* and *fat*; the fat content also makes its flour go rancid quickly, which is why it is traditionally milled in small quantities.
- *Foxtail and proso:* ~11 g protein; proso notably low in lysine, foxtail better balanced.

*Pharmacology and clinical:*
- *Glycaemic control* — millets have a lower glycaemic index than polished rice and refined wheat, and a modest clinical literature, largely Indian, reports improved postprandial glycaemia and some benefit in type 2 diabetes. *Modest, heterogeneous, and mostly small trials*; the effect is real in direction and oversold in magnitude.
- *Lipids and weight* — small studies suggest modest improvements; preliminary.
- *Goitrogenic C-glycosyl flavones* — the one well-characterized adverse pharmacology; see Viruddha. The mechanism (thyroid peroxidase inhibition) is established; the clinical significance is confined to iodine-deficient millet-staple populations.
- *Polyphenols and tannins* — antioxidant in vitro, and simultaneously the antinutrient fraction; the traditional processing reduces them, which trades some polyphenol content for better mineral absorption. *A genuine trade-off rather than a free benefit*, and usually reported as the latter.

*The honest summary on the "superfood" question.* The classical traditions that knew millets best — Ayurveda explicitly, Tibb in its "little nourishment" phrase, and China by preferring rice and wheat where they would grow — classed them as *second-rank grains*, and on their own terms they were not wrong: per calorie, millets nourish and build less than rice or wheat, which is what *laṅghana* and *qalīl al-ghidhāʾ* are saying. *The modern case for millets is a different case*, and a good one: lower glycaemic load, better micronutrient density, gluten-free, and above all *climate resilience in a warming, water-constrained world*. These are real advantages that the classical rankings did not weigh and had no reason to. *Both verdicts can stand*, and the entry declines to collapse them.

*Safe daily intake:* a staple food with *no toxicological ceiling* in ordinary mixed diets. The genuine limits:
- *Iodine-deficient populations with millet-dominant staple diets* — the goitrogen interaction; addressed by iodine sufficiency rather than by avoiding millet.
- *Kodo millet* — source and storage attention, for the ergot risk.
- *Vāta-type or depleted constitutions, in the classical reading* — cook with fat, do not use as a sole staple, and prefer the fermented and soaked preparations.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Composition figures vary between USDA FoodData Central and Indian food-composition tables (NIN/ICMR) and between species — verify, and do not apply one species' figures to another. Finger-millet calcium (~344 mg/100 g) is a widely cited and well-established figure, with the phytate/oxalate bioavailability qualification equally well established. Goitrogenic C-glycosyl flavones and thyroid peroxidase inhibition in pearl and finger millet, and the endemic-goitre association in iodine-deficient millet-staple populations: established in the endocrinology and nutrition literature. Kodua poisoning from _Claviceps paspali_ ergot contamination of _Paspalum scrobiculatum_: documented in Indian toxicology literature. Glycaemic and metabolic trial evidence: predominantly small Indian trials and meta-analyses — modest and heterogeneous; verify against current systematic reviews.]

